# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/004_common_subexpression_invariance.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_common_subexpression_invariance
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Repeated subexpressions producing identical results whether or not they are eliminated
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every repeated subexpression is evaluated over operands that stay inside the range of their
type, so no signed overflow occurs and no divisor is zero.  No subexpression has a side
effect, so sharing one cannot change the program's behaviour, and only printed values are
compared, never which subexpressions were shared.  Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
e1=96
e2=96
e1_eq_e2=1
e3=96 e1_eq_e3=1
product=9216
chain=380
nested=91
uexp1=428571432 uexp2=428571432 equal=1
branchy=96
END
